Here's a complete, production-ready error boundary. Error boundaries must be class components — `getDerivedStateFromError` and `componentDidCatch` have no hook equivalents.

## `ErrorBoundary.tsx`

```tsx
import { Component, type ErrorInfo, type ReactNode } from 'react';

export interface FallbackProps {
  error: Error;
  errorInfo: ErrorInfo | null;
  retry: () => void;
}

interface Props {
  children: ReactNode;
  /** Custom fallback renderer. Falls back to DefaultFallback if omitted. */
  fallback?: (props: FallbackProps) => ReactNode;
  /** Side-effect hook for reporting to Sentry/Datadog/etc. */
  onError?: (error: Error, errorInfo: ErrorInfo) => void;
  /** Called after a successful retry, so parents can reset related state. */
  onReset?: () => void;
  /**
   * When any value in this array changes, the boundary auto-resets.
   * Useful for clearing the error on route/param changes.
   */
  resetKeys?: unknown[];
}

interface State {
  error: Error | null;
  errorInfo: ErrorInfo | null;
}

const INITIAL_STATE: State = { error: null, errorInfo: null };

export class ErrorBoundary extends Component<Props, State> {
  state: State = INITIAL_STATE;

  /** Render phase: decide what to show. Must stay side-effect free. */
  static getDerivedStateFromError(error: Error): Partial<State> {
    return { error };
  }

  /** Commit phase: safe place for logging side effects. */
  componentDidCatch(error: Error, errorInfo: ErrorInfo): void {
    this.setState({ errorInfo });

    // Structured log — component stack is the part stack traces lack.
    console.error('[ErrorBoundary] Uncaught render error', {
      message: error.message,
      stack: error.stack,
      componentStack: errorInfo.componentStack,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    });

    // Never let the reporter itself crash the boundary.
    try {
      this.props.onError?.(error, errorInfo);
    } catch (reportingError) {
      console.error('[ErrorBoundary] onError handler threw', reportingError);
    }
  }

  componentDidUpdate(prevProps: Props): void {
    const { resetKeys } = this.props;
    if (!this.state.error || !resetKeys) return;

    const prevKeys = prevProps.resetKeys ?? [];
    const changed =
      prevKeys.length !== resetKeys.length ||
      resetKeys.some((key, i) => !Object.is(key, prevKeys[i]));

    if (changed) this.reset();
  }

  reset = (): void => {
    this.setState(INITIAL_STATE, () => this.props.onReset?.());
  };

  render(): ReactNode {
    const { error, errorInfo } = this.state;
    const { children, fallback } = this.props;

    if (error) {
      const fallbackProps: FallbackProps = { error, errorInfo, retry: this.reset };
      return fallback ? fallback(fallbackProps) : <DefaultFallback {...fallbackProps} />;
    }

    return children;
  }
}

function DefaultFallback({ error, retry }: FallbackProps) {
  return (
    <div role="alert" style={styles.container}>
      <h2 style={styles.heading}>Something went wrong</h2>
      <p style={styles.body}>
        This section failed to load. You can try again — the rest of the page is still usable.
      </p>

      {process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production' && (
        <pre style={styles.pre}>{error.message}</pre>
      )}

      <button type="button" onClick={retry} style={styles.button}>
        Try again
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

const styles: Record<string, React.CSSProperties> = {
  container: { padding: 24, border: '1px solid #f3c2c2', borderRadius: 8, background: '#fff6f6' },
  heading: { margin: '0 0 8px', fontSize: 18 },
  body: { margin: '0 0 16px', color: '#555' },
  pre: { margin: '0 0 16px', padding: 12, background: '#fff', overflowX: 'auto', fontSize: 12 },
  button: { padding: '8px 16px', cursor: 'pointer', borderRadius: 6, border: '1px solid #ccc' },
};
```

## Usage

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary
  onError={(error, info) => Sentry.captureException(error, { extra: info })}
  resetKeys={[userId]}          // auto-recover when the user changes
  onReset={() => queryClient.resetQueries()}
>
  <Dashboard />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

With a custom fallback:

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary fallback={({ error, retry }) => (
  <EmptyState title="Chart unavailable" detail={error.message} action={
    <Button onClick={retry}>Reload chart</Button>
  } />
)}>
  <RevenueChart />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

## Things worth knowing

**What it does *not* catch.** Error boundaries only catch errors thrown during render, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors of the subtree below them. They miss:
- event handlers (`onClick` etc.) — use try/catch there
- async code (`setTimeout`, promises, `fetch` callbacks)
- server-side rendering
- errors thrown in the boundary component itself

For async/event errors you need a separate path. A common trick is to re-throw into render so the boundary can see it:

```tsx
function useErrorHandler() {
  const [, setState] = useState();
  return useCallback((error: unknown) => {
    setState(() => { throw error; });
  }, []);
}
```

**Retry semantics.** `retry` clears the error state and re-mounts the subtree. If the underlying cause is still present (bad prop, cached bad response), it'll immediately error again. That's why `onReset` exists — use it to invalidate the query/cache that produced the bad data. For repeated failures, consider tracking a retry count in state and showing a "contact support" message after 2–3 attempts.

**Placement.** Granular beats global. Wrap each independently-failing region (sidebar, chart, comments) so one crash doesn't blank the whole page, plus one top-level boundary as a backstop. Since React 16, an uncaught render error unmounts the *entire* tree — so having at least one boundary is not optional.

**React 19 note.** Root-level `onUncaughtError` / `onCaughtError` options on `createRoot` let you centralize logging, and React 19 no longer double-logs caught errors to the console. The boundary API itself is unchanged.

**JavaScript version.** If you're not on TypeScript, delete the interfaces and type annotations — the class body is otherwise identical.

Error boundaries must be class components — `getDerivedStateFromError` and `componentDidCatch` have no hook equivalents. Here's a complete implementation:

**`ErrorBoundary.tsx`**

```tsx
import { Component, type ErrorInfo, type ReactNode } from 'react';

type FallbackProps = {
  error: Error;
  errorInfo: ErrorInfo | null;
  retry: () => void;
};

type Props = {
  children: ReactNode;
  /** Custom fallback. Receives the error and a retry callback. */
  fallback?: (props: FallbackProps) => ReactNode;
  /** Side-effect hook for your logging service. */
  onError?: (error: Error, errorInfo: ErrorInfo) => void;
  /** Called after a successful retry — useful for refetching data. */
  onReset?: () => void;
  /** When any value in this array changes, the boundary auto-resets. */
  resetKeys?: unknown[];
};

type State = {
  error: Error | null;
  errorInfo: ErrorInfo | null;
};

const initialState: State = { error: null, errorInfo: null };

export class ErrorBoundary extends Component<Props, State> {
  state: State = initialState;

  // Runs during the render phase: must be pure, only updates state.
  static getDerivedStateFromError(error: Error): Partial<State> {
    return { error };
  }

  // Runs during the commit phase: safe place for side effects like logging.
  componentDidCatch(error: Error, errorInfo: ErrorInfo): void {
    this.setState({ errorInfo });

    // Structured log — component stack is the part a plain window.onerror misses.
    console.error('[ErrorBoundary] Uncaught render error', {
      message: error.message,
      stack: error.stack,
      componentStack: errorInfo.componentStack,
    });

    this.props.onError?.(error, errorInfo);
  }

  componentDidUpdate(prevProps: Props): void {
    const { resetKeys } = this.props;
    if (!this.state.error || !resetKeys) return;

    const changed =
      prevProps.resetKeys?.length !== resetKeys.length ||
      resetKeys.some((key, i) => !Object.is(key, prevProps.resetKeys?.[i]));

    if (changed) this.reset();
  }

  reset = (): void => {
    this.setState(initialState);
    this.props.onReset?.();
  };

  render(): ReactNode {
    const { error, errorInfo } = this.state;
    if (!error) return this.props.children;

    if (this.props.fallback) {
      return this.props.fallback({ error, errorInfo, retry: this.reset });
    }

    return (
      <div role="alert" className="error-boundary">
        <h2>Something went wrong.</h2>
        <p>{error.message}</p>
        {import.meta.env?.DEV && errorInfo && (
          <pre style={{ overflow: 'auto', fontSize: 12 }}>
            {errorInfo.componentStack}
          </pre>
        )}
        <button type="button" onClick={this.reset}>
          Try again
        </button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}
```

**Usage**

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary
  resetKeys={[userId]}
  onError={(error, info) =>
    Sentry.captureException(error, {
      contexts: { react: { componentStack: info.componentStack } },
    })
  }
  onReset={() => queryClient.resetQueries()}
>
  <Dashboard userId={userId} />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

A few things worth knowing before you ship this:

**Retry only works if the error was transient.** `reset()` re-renders the same children. If the cause is deterministic — a bad prop, a null field in cached data — it will throw again immediately and the user gets a flicker loop. That's what `resetKeys` and `onReset` are for: change the input or clear the cache that produced the error, don't just re-mount identical state. For a hard guarantee, keep a retry counter in state and swap to a permanent "contact support" fallback after 2–3 attempts.

**It does not catch everything.** Error boundaries only catch errors thrown during render, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors of the subtree below them. Not caught: event handlers, `setTimeout`/`requestAnimationFrame` callbacks, async code (rejected promises), server-side rendering, and errors thrown in the boundary itself. Those need `try/catch` at the call site plus a global `window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', ...)` listener.

**Placement matters more than the implementation.** One boundary at the app root turns any error into a blank page. Wrap independently-failing regions instead — each route, each dashboard widget, each panel — so a broken chart doesn't take down the nav. Boundaries nest, so a coarse root-level one as a backstop plus fine-grained ones inside is the usual arrangement.

**Development is noisier than production.** In dev, React re-throws caught errors to the console and the overlay appears on top of your fallback — the boundary is working, the overlay just hides it. Dismiss it to see your UI. In React 19, `createRoot` also accepts `onCaughtError` / `onUncaughtError` options if you'd rather centralize logging at the root instead of per-boundary.

If you're already using `react-error-boundary` (the de-facto community package), it gives you this plus `useErrorBoundary()` for manually forwarding async errors into the nearest boundary — worth a look before maintaining your own.

