Notes:

- `getDerivedStateFromError` set state, `componentDidCatch` do side effect (log). Two hooks, two jobs.
- Retry clear error, remount children. If children throw again from same bad prop, loop. Add `resetKeys` prop or reset upstream state too.
- Catch only render, lifecycle, constructor. NOT event handlers, async, setTimeout, SSR. Those need try/catch.
- Dev mode React still log error to console — expected, double log.
- Place boundaries per route or per widget. One top-level boundary = whole app blank on small failure.

Error boundary must be class component. Hooks no catch render errors.

```tsx
import { Component, type ErrorInfo, type ReactNode } from 'react';

type FallbackProps = {
  error: Error;
  retry: () => void;
};

type Props = {
  children: ReactNode;
  fallback?: (props: FallbackProps) => ReactNode;
  onError?: (error: Error, info: ErrorInfo) => void;
  /** When any value changes, the boundary resets automatically. */
  resetKeys?: unknown[];
};

type State = {
  error: Error | null;
};

export class ErrorBoundary extends Component<Props, State> {
  state: State = { error: null };

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error: Error): State {
    return { error };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error: Error, info: ErrorInfo) {
    console.error('[ErrorBoundary] render error', {
      message: error.message,
      stack: error.stack,
      componentStack: info.componentStack,
    });
    this.props.onError?.(error, info);
  }

  componentDidUpdate(prevProps: Props) {
    const { resetKeys } = this.props;
    if (!this.state.error || !resetKeys) return;

    const prevKeys = prevProps.resetKeys ?? [];
    const changed =
      prevKeys.length !== resetKeys.length ||
      resetKeys.some((key, i) => !Object.is(key, prevKeys[i]));

    if (changed) this.reset();
  }

  reset = () => {
    this.setState({ error: null });
  };

  render() {
    const { error } = this.state;
    if (!error) return this.props.children;

    if (this.props.fallback) {
      return this.props.fallback({ error, retry: this.reset });
    }

    return (
      <div role="alert" className="error-boundary">
        <h2>Something went wrong.</h2>
        <p>{error.message}</p>
        <button type="button" onClick={this.reset}>
          Try again
        </button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}
```

Use:

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary
  resetKeys={[userId]}
  onError={(error, info) => reportToSentry(error, info)}
  fallback={({ error, retry }) => (
    <FallbackCard message={error.message} onRetry={retry} />
  )}
>
  <Profile userId={userId} />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

Key points:
- `getDerivedStateFromError` set fallback state. `componentDidCatch` do side effect (log/report).
- Retry only clear state. If same bad prop still there, error throw again. That why `resetKeys` — reset when input change.
- Boundary catch child render, lifecycle, constructor only. Not catch: event handlers, `setTimeout`, async code, SSR, error thrown in boundary itself. Those need try/catch or `window.onerror`.
- Put boundary per route/widget, not one at app root only. Granular = rest of UI survive.

Error boundary must be class component. Hooks no catch render error.

```tsx
import { Component, type ErrorInfo, type ReactNode } from 'react';

type FallbackProps = {
  error: Error;
  retry: () => void;
};

type Props = {
  children: ReactNode;
  fallback?: (props: FallbackProps) => ReactNode;
  onError?: (error: Error, info: ErrorInfo) => void;
  /** Change this value to auto-reset the boundary (e.g. route key). */
  resetKey?: unknown;
};

type State = {
  error: Error | null;
};

export class ErrorBoundary extends Component<Props, State> {
  state: State = { error: null };

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error: Error): State {
    return { error };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error: Error, info: ErrorInfo): void {
    console.error('[ErrorBoundary]', {
      message: error.message,
      stack: error.stack,
      componentStack: info.componentStack,
    });
    this.props.onError?.(error, info);
  }

  componentDidUpdate(prevProps: Props): void {
    if (this.state.error && prevProps.resetKey !== this.props.resetKey) {
      this.reset();
    }
  }

  reset = (): void => {
    this.setState({ error: null });
  };

  render(): ReactNode {
    const { error } = this.state;
    if (!error) return this.props.children;

    if (this.props.fallback) {
      return this.props.fallback({ error, retry: this.reset });
    }

    return (
      <div role="alert">
        <h2>Something went wrong</h2>
        <pre>{error.message}</pre>
        <button type="button" onClick={this.reset}>
          Retry
        </button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}
```

Usage:

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary
  resetKey={location.pathname}
  onError={(err, info) => reportToSentry(err, info)}
  fallback={({ error, retry }) => (
    <FallbackCard message={error.message} onRetry={retry} />
  )}
>
  <Dashboard />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

Key points:
- `getDerivedStateFromError` set state, `componentDidCatch` log side effect. Two hooks needed, different phases.
- Retry clear error, children remount, render retried. If cause persist (bad prop, bad cached data), error throw again immediately. Fix source too — pair retry with refetch.
- `resetKey` auto-clear on route change. Without it, stale error stick after navigate.
- Catch no: event handlers, async code, SSR, errors thrown in boundary itself. Wrap those with try/catch.
- Dev mode: React still log error to console after catch. Expected, no double-report.
